  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nHow is the minimum corporate income tax (MCIT) computed and when does it apply to a domestic corporation?\n\n### Short Answer\nThe minimum corporate income tax (MCIT) is computed at a rate of 2% of the gross income as of the end of the taxable year, applicable starting from the fourth taxable year following the year in which the corporation commenced its business operations, provided that the MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax for that year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **RR 9-1998**: This regulation provides further details on the implementation of the MCIT.\n\n### Legal Interpretation\n\n### Practical Application\nDomestic corporations should monitor their gross income and regular corporate income tax liabilities closely, especially as they approach the fourth year of operations. If the MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax, they must pay the MCIT. Additionally, any excess MCIT paid can be carried forward and credited against the normal income tax for the next three taxable years. Corporations facing financial difficulties may seek relief from the MCIT under certain conditions as authorized by the Secretary of Finance.",
